Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 22:07:54 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 58/71] Run a here-document under bash when no shell was
 requested

PowerShell reserves `<<` as a redirection operator and never implemented input
redirection, so a here-document is a parse error before the command runs:
`python - <<'PY'` reports "The '<' operator is reserved for future use." No
PowerShell invocation can be asking for one, which makes the shell choice
unambiguous whenever a command carries a here-document.

The model already knows this half the time. Across 15 rollouts (814 exec calls)
eight commands outside `apply_patch` carried a here-document: the five that
passed `shell: "bash"` all succeeded, and the three that omitted it all failed.
Resolve bash for those three instead of handing back a syntax error.

Detection asks `codex_shell_command::bash`, the tree-sitter grammar the
workspace already ships, for a `heredoc_redirect` node. Scanning for `<<`
cannot separate a redirection from the same two characters inside a quoted
body, and `Set-Content out.cpp -Value 'std::cout << x'` is a valid PowerShell
command that has to keep running there.

The fallback declines in three cases, each leaving the command in the session
shell exactly as before: the model named a shell itself, no bash is installed,
or the first `bash` on PATH is one of Windows' own shims. `System32\bash.exe`
and the `WindowsApps` alias launch WSL, whose filesystem shares nothing with
the Windows cwd the command runs in, so routing there would be worse than the
parse error it replaces.

Nothing else changes: `apply_patch` is intercepted before shell selection, the
sandbox sees a `ShellType::Bash` it already produces when the model passes
`shell` itself, and no prompt text was touched.

